    %% CLAIM C1 | The Calvin cycle, bio synthetic phase, dark reactions, or photosynthetic carbon reduction (PCR) cycle of photosynthesis is a series of chemical reactions that convert carbon dioxide and hydrogen-carrier compounds into glucose. | START
    %% CLAIM C2 | The Calvin cycle is present in all photosynthetic eukaryotes and also many photosynthetic bacteria. | START
    %% CLAIM C3 | In plants, these reactions occur in the stroma, the fluid-filled region of a chloroplast outside the thylakoid membranes. | START
    %% CLAIM C4 | The Calvin cycle thus happens when light is available independent of the kind of photosynthesis (C3 carbon fixation, C4 carbon fixation, and crassulacean acid metabolism (CAM)); CAM plants store malic acid in their vacuoles every night and release it by day to make this process work. | D2
    %% CLAIM C5 | Though it is also called the "dark reaction", the Calvin cycle does not occur in the dark or during nighttime. | D2
    %% CLAIM C6 | The enzyme RuBisCO catalyses the carboxylation of ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate (RuBP) a 5-carbon compound, by carbon dioxide (a total of 6 carbons) in a two-step reaction. | C1
    %% CLAIM C7 | The product of the first step is enediol-enzyme complex that can capture CO 2 or O 2. | C1
    %% CLAIM C8 | The CO 2 that is captured by enediol in second step produces an unstable six-carbon compound called 2-carboxy 3-keto 1,5-biphosphoribotol (CKABP) (or 3-keto-2-carboxyarabinitol 1,5-bisphosphate) that immediately splits into 2 molecules of 3-phosphoglycerate (also written as 3-phosphoglyceric acid, PGA, 3PGA, or 3-PGA), a 3-carbon compound. | D1
    %% CLAIM C9 | RuBisCO also reacts competitively with O 2 instead of CO 2 in photorespiration. | D1
    %% CLAIM C10 | The enzyme phosphoglycerate kinase catalyses the phosphorylation of 3-PGA by ATP (which was produced in the light-dependent stage). | R1
    %% CLAIM C11 | The enzyme glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate dehydrogenase catalyses the reduction of 1,3BPGA by NADPH (which is another product of the light-dependent stage). | R1
    %% CLAIM C12 | Five G3P molecules produce three RuBP molecules, using up three molecules of ATP. | G1
    %% CLAIM C13 | Triose phosphate isomerase converts one of the G3P reversibly into dihydroxyacetone phosphate (DHAP), also a 3-carbon molecule. | G1
    %% CLAIM C14 | R5P is converted into ribulose-5-phosphate (Ru5P, RuP) by phosphopentose isomerase. | G2
    %% CLAIM C15 | Xu5P is converted into RuP by phosphopentose epimerase. | G2
    %% CLAIM C16 | Finally, phosphoribulokinase (another plant-unique enzyme of the pathway) phosphorylates RuP into RuBP, ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate, completing the Calvin cycle. | G2
    %% CLAIM C17 | Thus, of six G3P produced, five are used to make three RuBP (5C) molecules (totaling 15 carbons), with only one G3P available for subsequent conversion to hexose. | O1
    %% CLAIM C18 | Surplus G3P can also be used to form other carbohydrates such as starch, sucrose, and cellulose, depending on what the plant needs. | O1
    %% CLAIM C19 | Photorespiration turns RuBP into 3-PGA and 2-phosphoglycolate, a 2-carbon molecule that can be converted via glycolate and glyoxylate to glycine. | P1
    %% CLAIM C20 | Serine can be converted back to 3-phosphoglycerate. | P1
    %% CLAIM C21 | In the dark, plants instead release sucrose into the phloem from their starch reserves to provide energy for the plant. | NDARK
